WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.270
There's an old expression: curiosity killed
the cat.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.340
It's a quick way of saying that if you ask
too many questions, you may not like the answers

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.760
— or troubles — you get as a result.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.870
This particular adage would certainly serve
viewers of the John Wick series well: ask

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.429
too many questions, and your head might start
to hurt.

00:00:17.429 --> 00:00:21.769
Here are some (but not all) of the biggest
unanswered questions that we came up with

00:00:21.769 --> 00:00:24.000
by the end of John Wick: Chapter 3: Parabellum.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.710
And if you haven't seen the movie yet, plug
some bullets into your ears — major spoilers

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:29.720
ahead.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.000
Each installment of the John Wick saga seems
to introduce more important characters from

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.310
John's past, featuring some pretty big time
Hollywood actors playing the parts.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:42.800
While these new faces fill in some blanks,
they certainly raise plenty of questions of

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:43.850
their own.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:48.050
Sofia, played by Halle Berry, probably has
some of the most memorable and exciting scenes

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.050
with Wick.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.690
The two team up (along with her very good
dogs) to get John on the road to meeting the

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:56.940
Elder of the High Table, and kill a bunch
of jerks together in the process.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.300
When all is said and done, Sofia's debt to
John is paid — and she seems to think that

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.710
he actually owes her another favor now.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:07.530
But while just about everyone else who comes
into contact with Wick throughout his period

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:12.600
of being excommunicado gets punished by the
Adjudicator or Zero and his ninja goons, we

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.729
never see the consequences of Sofia's decision
to help John.

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:19.170
Will the High Table exact vengeance on Sofia
in the next film?

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.499
Will she be forced out of her own hotel management
position as well?

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:25.680
Will her mysterious hidden daughter come into
play as a result?

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.159
The biggest source of conflict within John
Wick: Chapter 3 was the friction between the

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:33.259
characters and the rules dictating their secret
society.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:38.140
It all starts in John Wick: Chapter 2, when
John breaks the rules inside the Continental

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:43.069
by killing Santino D'Antonio, the crime lord
who'd tricked John into killing his sister

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:47.880
Gianna D'Antonio — all so Santino could
ascend to her seat on the High Table.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.100
Everyone who helps John afterward is likewise
punished for breaking yet more rules.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.999
Meanwhile, John travels across the world to
meet with the Elder, who apparently runs the

00:01:55.999 --> 00:02:00.520
High Table, and who tasks him with killing
Winston, the Continental Manager, to make

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.549
things right.

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:04.649
But let's be real: how do you get to run the
High Table?

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:07.350
And just how do all these crazy rules get
decided?

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.390
For a group of lawless murderers, they sure
do seem to be sticklers for regulations.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:14.650
Moreover, how does someone get a job with
the High Table?

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.629
The Adjudicator sure seemed pretty young to
have such a powerful position.

00:02:18.629 --> 00:02:22.580
Were representatives from the High Table showing
up at colleges, offering exciting jobs in

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.849
rule enforcement among secret assassin societies?

00:02:25.849 --> 00:02:29.799
And how come John never managed to climb the
corporate ladder and get his own seat considering

00:02:29.799 --> 00:02:32.790
how he's literally the greatest assassin who
ever lived?

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.519
You'd think they'd give him a seat just to
give the other million or so assassins a chance

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.920
to make a buck.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.950
The end of John Wick Chapter 2 features a
pretty cool moment.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.670
In a park in New York City, Winston declares
John excommunicado and the price on his head

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:48.420
goes worldwide.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.769
After that, tons of phones start ringing all
around John and his dog, seeming to indicate

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:57.270
that in addition to all of the assassins John
faced already in the movie, all these people

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.560
are likewise hired guns.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.140
John Wick Chapter 3 continues with this thread,
as would-be badasses all over the world spot

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.670
John out in the wild and try to snuff him
out.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:11.329
But as the film continues, hardly a moment
goes by where John isn't beset by highly trained

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:15.390
killers just, like, hanging around, waiting
for him to stroll by.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.920
It raises a pretty big question: just how
many people in this universe earn a living

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.750
wage as an assassin?

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:25.840
Are all of these people just trading the same
pile of money back and forth as each one of

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.989
them kills some other assassin for one reason
or another?

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:31.189
Shouldn't they have run out of assassins by
now?

00:03:31.189 --> 00:03:36.000
And do literally all of them have side jobs
as subway violinists, dog walkers, or cab

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.000
drivers?

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.480
New York is expensive — you gotta hussle
if you want to make your rent.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.760
Chances are good most landlords won't take
fancy gold coins in lieu of a check.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:50.680
In June 2017, news hit the web that the filmmakers
behind the John Wick franchise had plans to

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:54.939
develop a TV show set in the same universe
called The Continental.

00:03:54.939 --> 00:03:59.090
The show will reportedly be set in the New
York-based hotel of the same name, though

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:04.290
it will supposedly feature a new cast of characters
that will only somewhat intersect with the

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.580
characters we've met in the movie franchise.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:10.300
Knowing all this helps make sense of the turn
of events towards the end of the film, when

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:14.620
the Adjudicator agrees to allow Winston to
retain control of the Continental, and to

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.209
restore its status as neutral ground for the
world's assassins.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:22.600
You can't have a show about the assassin hotel
if the hotel goes out of business, right?

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:27.020
And yet, Ian McShane, the actor who plays
Winston, has reportedly said that he has no

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.090
plans to appear in the show.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:32.250
So will the show recast the part to keep the
character around?

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:36.280
Or will he conveniently be on vacation out
in the country somewhere throughout the show's

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:37.280
run?

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.130
Maybe he'll just throw out his back, and won't
ever leave the couch in the Continental's

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.210
back room, where no one can see him.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.600
Yeah, that's probably what they'll do.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.480
Now, here's the biggest question of all.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:51.919
After agreeing to let Winston keep control
over his hotel, the Adjudicator declares that

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:56.460
John Wick remains a big problem: he's still
excommunicado for breaking the Continental's

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:00.500
rules in the previous movie, and he hasn't
fulfilled his mission to kill Winston per

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.310
his deal with the Elder.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:06.889
In response, Winston grabs a gun and shoots
John repeatedly — pointedly aiming for his

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:11.550
bullet-proof suit, rather than his head — and
Wick falls off the roof of the hotel, bouncing

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:16.120
off of a couple fire escapes before he thuds
onto the ground like Wile E. Coyote.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.690
And just like Wile E. Coyote, Wick is still
breathing after his monumental fall.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.389
By the end of the film, he and the Bowery
King vowed to take revenge on the High Table

00:05:24.389 --> 00:05:28.240
in the next film, John Wick Chapter 4: Who
Let the Dogs Out.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.300
But when Winston learns that Wick is possibly
still alive, he doesn't seem all that surprised.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.810
And yet, by any reasonable measure of logic
and physics, even in a world as cartoonishly

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:42.819
violent as the one John Wick inhabits, Wick
for sure should have been a smear on the pavement.

00:05:42.819 --> 00:05:46.590
Does Winston know something we don't about
John Wick's skeleton and organs?

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.040
Is he secretly a cyborg?

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.600
Or is this really all just taking place in
the Matrix, and now Neo and Morpheus are going

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.729
to team up to take it to the machines one
last time?

00:05:55.729 --> 00:05:58.759
Because this is for sure all happening in
the Matrix, right?

00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:00.099
Fingers crossed.

